@dataclasses.dataclass(frozen=True)
class BlastArgs:
    """What your Bots can do UNATTENDED, and how bad it could get.

    Measured on this account: every one of 13 Bots reaches all 142 write-shaped tool instances,
    because the effective MCP config is ACCOUNT-level with no per-Bot scope — you cannot reduce
    one Bot's reach without reducing every Bot's. 130 of those 142 are named by an auto-review
    rule; the 12 that are not are all CREATIONS, because the account's rules name sending,
    paying, publishing, deleting and changing production, and never name creating.

    The worst cell is 2 of 13: reaches a write-shaped tool no rule names, runs unattended on an
    enabled routine, and cannot notify its operator.

    Two figures are HEURISTIC and labelled as such wherever they surface. The honest signal
    exists — MCP tool definitions carry `annotations.readOnlyHint` / `destructiveHint`, the
    server's own answer — but `bin/gb-deployment-audit.py` records only the tool name and a
    count and discards the rest, so it is not on disk. Retiring the heuristic is an audit fix,
    not something this verb can work around.
    """

    json: bool = arg(help="machine-readable envelope on stdout")
    selftest: bool = arg(help="prove this module's properties")


